##

  1. Markdown

Markdown is a lightweight markup language used to format text. Its primary
purpose is to make writing and reading formatted documents simple, especially
for documentation, readmes, and technical content.

**File Extension:** Markdown files typically have the extension .md or
.markdown

**Common Use Cases:**

```bash
* Documentation: Describing how a project works.
 * Readmes: Providing an overview of a repository.
 * Changelogs: Tracking changes in projects.
 * Wikis: Collaborative project documentation.
```

**Open Preview in VS-code:**

When you open the readme or markdown file in the VS-code, in the top right
side of VS-code you can see such an icon, click on icon readme file preview
will be opened.

![Markdown_b_1](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_1.png)
![Markdown_b_2](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_2.png)

##

  2. Headings

**Example:**

To create a heading, add one to six # symbols before your heading text. The
number of # you will determine the hierarchy level and typeface size of the
heading.

```bash
# Heading 1
## Heading 2
### Heading 3
#### Heading 4
##### Heading 5
###### Heading 6
```

**Output:**

![Markdown_b_3](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_3.png)

##

  3. Paragraphs

**Example:**

```bash
This is a paragraph.

Leave a lank line to separate paragraphs.
```

**Output:**

![Markdown_b_4](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_4.png)

##

  4. Text Styles

**Example:**

```bash
**Bold Text** 
*Italic Text* 
***Bold and Italic***
```

**Output:**

![Markdown_b_5](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_5.png)

##

  5. Lists

**Unordered List:**

```bash
- Item 1
- Item 2
- Sub-item
```

**Output:**

![Markdown_b_6](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_6.png)

**Ordered List:**

```bash
1. Step 1
2. Step 2
  1. Sub-step
```

**Output:**

![Markdown_b_7](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_7.png)

##

  6. Code Blocks

**Inline Code:**

```bash
Use `backticks` for inline code.
```

**Output:**

![Markdown_b_8](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_8.png)

**Quoting code:**

```bash
Some basic Git commands are:
```
git status
git add
git commit
```
```

**Output:**

![Markdown_b_9](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_9.png)

##

  7. Links

**Example:**

```bash
[Visit GitHub](https://github.com)
```

**Output:**

![Markdown_b_10](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_10.png)

##

  8. Images

**Example:**

```systemverilog
![GitHub-Logo](https://github.githubassets.com/images/modules/logos_page/GitHub-Mark.png)

![GitHub-Logo](path-to-file/GitHub-Mark.png)
            
            
<p align="center">
<img width=200 height=200 src='image-link or path-of-image'>
</p>
```

**Output:**

![Markdown_b_11](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_11.png)

##

  9. Tables

**Example:**

```bash
| Feature    | Description             |
|------------|-------------------------|
| Simplicity | Easy to write and read  |
| Versatile  | Works in many platforms |
```

**Output:**

![Markdown_b_12](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_12.png)

##

  10. Checklists

**Example:**

```bash
- [x] Task 1 (Completed)
- [ ] Task 2 (Pending)
```

**Output:**

![Markdown_b_13](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_13.png)

##

  11. Emojis

**Example:**

```bash
Use emojis for fun: :smile: :rocket: :+1:
```

**Output:**

![Markdown_b_14](/meds-ee-uet/images/posts/Markdown_b_14.png)

